And these divine powers govern another sixfold sequence.

Childhood.

Youth.

Old age.

Death.

Rebirth.

Liberation.

Now look at that.

The whole career of an embodied being is contemplated within the downward vital movement.

Birth.

Development.

Decline.

Death.

And the possibilities of continued existence or liberation.

It’s the same logic we’ve encountered throughout the chapter.

Cosmic cycles and individual cycles are not treated as fundamentally unrelated processes.

They express different patterns of the same consciousness.

Now, let’s move to the next vital energy.

And this one’s particularly interesting.

18. Samāna — the cosmic traffic controller
Samāna.

The equalising vital energy.

According to this subtle-body system, it travels through ten channels associated with the Heart.

Nāḍīs.

And distributes the body’s nutritive essences and other vital substances.

Now, some of those movements are directed toward the eight directions.

Others proceed upward and downward through three principal channels.

You may already know their names.

Iḍā.

Piṅgalā.

Suṣumnā.

The left.

The right.

And the central channel.

And Abhinavagupta gives a rather detailed account of how the vital movement alternates through them in relation to the external day.

Let’s take an equinox.

Viṣuvat.

At dawn, the movement begins through the central channel.

Madhyamārga.

For one and one-quarter ghaṭikās.

That’s thirty minutes in ordinary timekeeping.

Then comes a series of alternations.

Left.

Right.

Left.

Right.

Left.

Five passages.

Saṅkrāntis.

The account associates each passage with nine hundred breath movements over approximately one hour.

Then, at midday, another central conjunction occurs.

And another five passages follow.

Right.

Left.

Right.

Left.

Right.

The arrangement repeats through the night.

And through this pattern, the text establishes twelve passages in the day and twelve in the night.

Now, why the interest in equinoxes?

Because an equinox represents a balance between day and night.

And this balanced external condition is reflected in the model of internal vital movement.

As days lengthen and shorten, the proportions of the passages are also understood to change.

But here’s a detail worth noticing.

In the outward-and-inward movement of ordinary prāṇa, we have two complementary directions.

But samāna is treated as a single equalising vital movement.

So Abhinavagupta says that two years can be contemplated in one movement of samāna.

And the higher multiplications into twelve years and beyond apply here too.

Now, we’re not measuring actual nasal airflow in a laboratory.

We’re examining a traditional map of subtle energy and cosmic correspondences.

Its purpose is contemplative.

And the other two vital energies have their own relationships with time.

19. Udāna and Vyāna — time gets increasingly subtle
First:

Udāna.

The ascending energy.

Its movement extends toward the dvādaśānta.

But here, the temporal process is characterised in terms of:

Spanda-mātra.

Pulsation alone.

The same general principles of temporal correspondence apply, but in an increasingly subtle mode.

Now comes:

Vyāna.

The pervasive vital energy.

And this is particularly remarkable.

Because vyāna is all-pervading.

Vyāpaka.

So it isn’t confined to an ordinary successive movement in the same way.

Akrama.

Nonsequential.

But even here, time can arise through a subtle bursting forth.

Sūkṣmocchalattā.

A minute emergence.

And now consider the philosophical point.

Time doesn’t have to be understood merely as a succession of gross, measurable external events.

Even within the increasingly subtle manifestations of consciousness, there are modes of appearance and differentiation.

And these can be contemplated as time.

Now we’ve examined all five vital energies.

Excellent.

Surely we’re finished?

No.

Because Abhinavagupta now returns to an old favourite.

The Sanskrit alphabet.

20. The alphabet is running its own cosmic clock
Remember Chapter Three?

All those divine vowels?

All those consonants?

The entire Sanskrit alphabet interpreted as a manifestation of Śakti?

Well, apparently the letters also possess a temporal unfolding.

Varṇodaya.

The arising of phonemes.

And Abhinavagupta distinguishes two kinds of emergence.

One that occurs without deliberate effort.

Ayatnaja.

And one that depends upon effort.

Yatnaja.

Now, the spontaneous emergence of the phonemic groups is assigned to the breath-cycle according to a regular sequence.

At the equinox, he describes the arising of phonemic groups at intervals of half a prahara.

And each individual phoneme corresponds to 216 breaths.

Or thirty-six caṣakas.

In the conventional conversion, that’s fourteen minutes and twenty-four seconds.

Now, I’m not suggesting your tongue is automatically reciting the entire Sanskrit alphabet every quarter of an hour.

We’re talking about the phonemic order being contemplated within the rhythm of vital energy.

And that’s the effortless version.

But there’s another.

A deliberate practice.

Mantrodaya.

The arising of mantra.

And here’s where things become especially interesting.

21. Your mantra is a waterwheel
Right.

Imagine one of those old waterwheels.

Buckets arranged around a wheel.

Round it goes.

One bucket descends.

Another rises.

Water is lifted.

Then another bucket.

Then another.

An ongoing cycle.

And Abhinavagupta uses precisely this kind of image.

Araghaṭṭa.

A water-lifting wheel.

Now imagine your mantra.

You’re contemplating it continuously.

Its sounds.

Its emergence.

Its power.

The deity it expresses.

And through sustained anusandhāna — contemplative continuity — the awareness of the mantra becomes increasingly unified.

Not merely repeating words like a malfunctioning parrot.

But maintaining an unbroken thread of recognition.

Eventually, the practitioner is said to attain identity with the mantra-deity.

Mantradevatayā saha tādātmyam.

Identity with the divinity of the mantra.

And this can be contemplated throughout day and night.

Now, we’ve already established the traditional count of 21,600 breaths per day.

And the mantra’s arising can be related to this count.

For instance, within a wheel of 108 divisions, each division corresponds to two hundred breath movements.

Because:

21,600 divided by 108 is 200.

Very neat.

Now, don’t get distracted by the arithmetic.

The really important thing is what happens when the practitioner becomes established in the gross and subtle movements of prāṇa.

Because something extraordinary occurs.

Time is swallowed.

Kālagrāsa.

22. Śiva eats time
Now listen carefully.

As the movements of prāṇa subside and temporal differentiation is absorbed, the practitioner recognises one complete consciousness.

Sampūrṇam ekaṃ saṃvedanam.

An undivided awareness.

And this consciousness is full of innumerable powers.

Citra-śakti-nirbhara.

Now, ordinarily you experience one cognition followed by another.

You see a flower.

Then a tree.

Then you hear a bird.

Then you remember something.

Then you think about lunch.

It’s all happening in time.

And you assume that the different objects themselves are what fundamentally divide cognition.

But Abhinavagupta proposes something subtler.

The distinction between cognitions arises through temporal differentiation, not simply through the differences between their objects.

Kālabheda eva saṃvedanabhedakaḥ, na vedyabhedaḥ.

Now, let’s investigate.

Imagine you’re standing at the summit of a mountain.

You’ve got an enormous view.

Rivers.

Forests.

Villages.

Mountains.

Clouds.

Countless different objects.

But all can be apprehended within a single broad field of awareness.

The mere presence of multiple objects doesn’t necessarily require a separate cognition for every individual thing.

That’s important.

Because cognition has a temporal unity.

A duration over which it exists as one cognition.

Kṣaṇa.

A moment.

And now we get a very subtle argument.

23. How many thoughts are you having right now?
Right.

Imagine you’re thinking a sentence.

“I am the supreme consciousness.”

Lovely sentence.

Very good.

Now, pronounce it internally.

“I…”

“Am…”

“The…”

“Supreme…”

“Consciousness.”

These words arise successively.

One after another.

Even the phonetic components of the words unfold in time.

Yet you apprehend the sentence as one meaningful thought.

Now here’s the problem.

If every successive phonetic moment necessarily constituted an entirely separate cognition, how could you ever have one coherent conceptual thought?

You’d just have an endless series of disconnected fragments.

“I.”

Gone.

“Am.”

Gone.

“The.”

Gone.

Absolutely useless.

You’d never understand a complete sentence.

So there must be a unity of cognition across the successive elements.

And Abhinavagupta even appeals to Sanskrit grammatical analysis, citing Pāṇini on the temporal divisions of vocal accent.

Yes.

He’s using phonetics to investigate the unity of consciousness.

Typical Abhinavagupta.

One minute you’re discussing cosmic dissolution.

The next you’re analysing half a short vowel.

But the argument is remarkably precise.

As long as another distinct pulsation of cognition has not arisen, the previous cognition remains one.

Tasmāt spandāntaraṃ yāvan noditaṃ tāvad ekam eva jñānam.

And now comes the beautiful conclusion.

24. How eighty-one ideas can become one recognition
Remember how we’ve been discussing distinctions?

Countless different powers.

Countless principles.

Countless expressions.

Well, Abhinavagupta now refers to the remembrance of eighty-one padas.

Through the apprehension of their many distinct attributes, a single conceptual understanding can be formed.

But its object is the supreme Lord.

Parameśvara.

And as the differentiated temporal structure of that cognition is absorbed, the cognition itself becomes free from conceptual division.

Avikalpa.

Now think about that.

We’ve come full circle.

Remember Śāktopāya?

The method of Śakti?

Correct conceptual understanding becomes the means through which limited conceptual understanding is overcome.

Well, here’s the same principle approached through the temporal unfolding of cognition.

The many expressions are contemplated.

Their differences are understood.

But the underlying consciousness is one.

And when their succession ceases to dominate awareness, the unity becomes evident.

Conceptual multiplicity is resolved in nonconceptual recognition.

Not because you’ve forcibly destroyed thought.

But because the awareness sustaining the entire sequence is recognised as more fundamental than its divisions.

And what did we establish at the beginning of the chapter?

That all time is an expression of Śiva’s own power.

Kālī-śakti.

So now the yogin can contemplate every possible temporal division within the movement of prāṇa.

Every day.

Every month.

Every year.

Every cosmic age.

Every creation.

Every dissolution.

The lifespan of Brahmā.

The lifespan of Viṣṇu.

The lifespan of Rudra.

All the way to those immeasurable periods beyond ordinary calculation.

And all of it is understood as arising within the pulsation of consciousness.

And then comes the actual recognition.

Ātmana eva pārameśvaryaṃ pratyabhijānan.

Recognising the supreme lordship of one’s own Self.

That’s the goal.

Not merely acquiring an extraordinary theory of time.

Not memorising the lifespans of cosmic gods.

Not becoming an expert in tantric numerology.

But recognising that the entire temporal order is a manifestation of the consciousness whose true nature is your own.

And Abhinavagupta says:

Mukta eva bhavati.

One becomes liberated.

And now we arrive at the final verse.

25. The concluding verse — you are the consciousness in which time appears
ORIGINAL SANSKRIT

संविद्रूपस्यात्मनः प्राणशक्तिंपश्यन्रूपं तत्रगं चातिकालम्।साकं सृष्टिस्थेमसंहारचक्रैःनित्योद्युक्तो भैरवीभावमेति॥
