Saṃvidrūpasyātmanaḥ prāṇaśaktiṃ

paśyan rūpaṃ tatragaṃ cātikālam.

Sākaṃ sṛṣṭi-sthema-saṃhāra-cakrair

nityodyukto bhairavībhāvam eti.

Right.

One final teaching.

Contemplate the power of prāṇa belonging to your own Self.

And what is the nature of that Self?

Saṃvidrūpa.

Consciousness itself.

Then recognise, within that vital power, the reality that transcends time.

Atikāla.

Beyond time.

Not something that lasts for an exceptionally long time.

Not an immortal object sitting inside eternity.

The very conscious reality within which duration and succession arise.

And contemplate it together with all the wheels of manifestation, maintenance and dissolution.

Sṛṣṭi.

Sthema.

Saṃhāra.

Creation.

Continuation.

Withdrawal.

All occurring within the power of consciousness.

And the practitioner who remains constantly intent upon this recognition?

Nityodyukta.

Ever engaged.

What does such a person attain?

Bhairavībhāva.

The state of Bhairava.

The supreme condition.

And that’s Chapter Six.

Now, consider what Abhinavagupta has actually done here.

He began with your breathing.

The most ordinary thing imaginable.

Then he showed how traditional cosmic measures can be contemplated within its movement.

A day.

A month.

A year.

Sixty years.

The four yugas.

The lifespan of Brahmā.

The greater cycles of Viṣṇu, Rudra and the higher divine principles.

And eventually the incalculable emergence and withdrawal of entire universes.

But at the end of all that?

He doesn’t tell you to become fascinated with cosmic arithmetic.

He points you back to the power through which time appears at all.

Saṃvit.

Consciousness.

And its pulsation.

Spanda.

Because the person who sees only the succession of events experiences themselves as living inside time.

But the yogin who recognises the underlying conscious reality sees time as a manifestation within that reality.

The universe unfolds in time. Time unfolds through Śakti. And Śakti is inseparable from Śiva.

And your own essential nature?

That very consciousness.

So there you are.

Sitting in your chair.

Breathing.

Perhaps making yourself a cup of tea.

And according to Abhinavagupta, the power through which that ordinary breath appears is the very power through which the innumerable cycles of cosmic creation and dissolution are manifested.

You’ve got the entire bloody universe arising and disappearing within the freedom of consciousness.

And you’re worried about being late for something.

Magnificent.

