Tantrasāra — Chapter 7
सप्तममाह्निकम् — Deśādhvaprakāśanam

The Path of Space: or, how Śiva has managed to fit an infinite number of universes, 118 divine worlds, and the entire cosmic hierarchy inside your bloody consciousness.

An original slang adaptation following the Sanskrit chapter, including its concluding verses and catalogues of worlds. The jokes and dialogue are invented. gretil.sub.uni-goettingen.de +1

Right then.

Last chapter we discussed time.

Kāla.

Absolutely ridiculous chapter.

We started with one little breath, somehow managed to fit sixty years inside it, continued multiplying until Brahmā’s entire lifespan looked like a bank holiday, and ultimately discovered that the whole temporal universe is merely a pulsation of consciousness.

Spanda.

Lovely.

But there’s a slight problem.

We’ve dealt with time.

What about space?

Because you might be perfectly happy accepting that time arises within consciousness.

Then you look around and think:

“Well, obviously the world is still out there, isn’t it?”

There’s a tree.

There’s a mountain.

There’s a house.

There’s an entire universe full of things that seem to exist independently of you.

And you appear to occupy one tiny little portion of it.

About six feet of human being, wandering around a planet in an inconceivably enormous cosmos.

So how on earth are you supposed to understand yourself as the all-pervading Lord?

Good question.

Today we’re going to examine Deśādhva.

The path of space.

And by the end of this chapter, you’ll see why even the most enormous conceivable universe is not outside Śiva.

Right.

Let’s get into it.

1. The entire spatial universe rests in consciousness
First, understand something.

All the extraordinary variety of forms you’ve been experiencing?

Every shape.

Every object.

Every location.

Every apparent distinction between one place and another.

All of it arises through a power of Śakti.

Mūrti-vaicitrya-ābhāsana-śakti.

The power that manifests the extraordinary diversity of forms.

Now, that is quite a mouthful.

So let’s break it down.

Mūrti — form.

Vaicitrya — diversity, variety.

Ābhāsana — manifestation, appearing.

Śakti — power.

In other words:

The power through which the whole bloody variety-show of existence appears.

And this entire spatial manifestation rests in:

Saṃvid.

Consciousness.

Now, don’t misunderstand me.

I’m not saying there’s a gigantic version of your human mind somewhere in outer space, containing tiny planets like marbles.

That would be an extremely peculiar interpretation.

I’m saying that the entire manifestation of spatial differences is grounded in consciousness.

And because of this, the whole cosmic path can be contemplated in various supports.

Such as:

Śūnya.

The void.

Buddhi.

The intellect.

Prāṇa.

Vital energy.

Then the subtle channels and wheels.

Nāḍī-cakra.

Then the body.

Deha.

And even things outside the body.

A liṅga.

An altar.

Sthaṇḍila.

An image of the deity.

Pratimā.

Now imagine that.

You could contemplate the entire universe in a ritual image.

Or within your body.

Or within your breath.

Or within your own intellectual understanding.

Why?

Because these supports and the universe contemplated within them aren’t ultimately independent of consciousness.

But here’s where it gets properly interesting.

We’re going to dissolve the entire thing.

2. The great cosmic reverse-unboxing
Right.

Imagine you’ve somehow managed to fit the entire universe into your body.

Every planet.

Every star.

Every divine world.

Every category of reality.

All thirty-six tattvas.

Quite cramped in there, isn’t it?

Well, now we’re going to dissolve the whole arrangement.

Vilāpya.

Dissolving.

Here’s the sequence.

First, contemplate the entire spatial path as dissolved into the body.

Then dissolve the body into prāṇa.

Then prāṇa into the intellect.

Then the intellect into the void.

And finally, the void into consciousness itself.

विश्वाध्वन् — Viśvādhvan

The entire manifested path

देह — Deha

The body

प्राण — Prāṇa

Vital energy

धी — Dhī

Intellect

शून्य — Śūnya

The void

संविद् — Saṃvid

Consciousness

Now, why this particular order?

Because we’re progressively withdrawing identification from increasingly subtle supports.

First you think:

“I’m in this body.”

Then you contemplate that body within the movement of vital energy.

Then that energy within the activity of understanding.

Then even conceptual understanding subsides into a condition of openness.

And ultimately, the void itself is recognised within consciousness.

Now, don’t imagine śūnya is the ultimate reality.

It’s not.

The void is still something that can be apprehended.

The supreme consciousness is the reality in which even that void appears.

And when you recognise the essential nature of all thirty-six tattvas through this process?

You enter:

Nirbhara-paripūrṇa-saṃvid.

Consciousness utterly replete with fullness.

Nothing missing.

Nothing standing independently outside it.

But here’s the crucial point.

You must recognise consciousness as both transcendent and immanent.

Viśvottīrṇa.

Beyond the universe.

And:

Viśvamaya.

Of the nature of the universe.

Now that might sound contradictory.

How can something be beyond the universe and also constitute the universe?

Well, because the supreme consciousness isn’t merely one item contained within the universe.

It’s the very reality through which every item appears.

So it isn’t exhausted by the particular forms of manifestation.

Yet those forms are not independent of it.

And if you don’t grasp both aspects, you’re going to make an unfortunate mistake.

3. Stop worshipping one department manager as the CEO of existence
Now, Abhinavagupta has a little warning.

Suppose you don’t understand the complete hierarchy of manifestation.

You contemplate some particular aspect of reality.

Perhaps an especially magnificent divine realm.

Or a particular deity.

And you decide:

“That’s it. That’s the absolute.”

But what if the thing you’ve identified is only one limited manifestation within a greater reality?

You’ve stopped too early.

You might take Brahmā as supreme.

Or Viṣṇu.

Or some other divine ruler operating within the domain of māyā.

And you mistake that limited position for the unsurpassable condition of Paramaśiva.

It’s like meeting the manager of a particular department and deciding he must be the ultimate authority over every possible company in every conceivable universe.

The bloke’s got an impressive office.

Lovely chair.

Probably a very important title.

But he’s still operating within a larger system.

And Abhinavagupta is quite emphatic about this.

You need to understand the actual structure of reality.

Prakriyā.

The systematic exposition of how the categories are arranged.

Now, this is an important word.

Because we’re not just drawing an interesting cosmic map to put on the wall.

We’re using a systematic understanding of reality to prevent ourselves from mistaking a limited category for the absolute.

And he cites the Svacchandatantra:

न प्रक्रियापरं ज्ञानम्

Na prakriyā-paraṃ jñānam.

There is no knowledge superior to knowledge of the systematic arrangement.

Within the context of this teaching, anyway.

And now you might reasonably ask:

“Well, Abhinava, what exactly is this arrangement?”

Ah.

Excellent.

Let’s start at the bottom.

Welcome to the cosmic property market.

4. The lowest universe is already absolutely enormous
First:

Pṛthivī-tattva.

The earth principle.

Number thirty-six.

The lowest of the thirty-six tattvas.

And how large is its domain?

Śata-koṭi-pravistīrṇa.

One hundred crores in extent.

That’s one billion yojanas in the traditional measure.

And what form does it take?

Brahmāṇḍa-golaka.

The spherical cosmic egg of Brahmā.

Now, don’t immediately start converting that into light-years and trying to compare it with the observable universe.

We’re discussing traditional tantric cosmography.

The point is the vast scale of the manifested realm.

And inside this cosmic egg, we’ve got quite a collection of places.

First, at the lower reaches:

Kālāgni.

The fire of time.

Then the hell realms.

Naraka.

Then the subterranean realms.

Pātāla.

Then the terrestrial world.

Pṛthivī.

Then the heavens.

Svarga.

And finally, at the upper end, the world of Brahmā.

Brahmaloka.

Now consider that.

You’ve got an entire cosmic hierarchy inside one single Brahmāṇḍa.

Infernal realms.

Subterranean worlds.

Human worlds.

Heavenly worlds.

And Brahmā himself.

Quite an establishment.

But then Abhinavagupta casually mentions something.

Outside the cosmic egg are one hundred Rudras.

One hundred.

And then he delivers another magnificent sentence.

Na ca brahmāṇḍānāṃ saṅkhyā vidyate.

There is no count of the cosmic eggs.

Innumerable universes.

Not one.

Not ten.

Not a hundred.

Innumerable.

And remember: all of this is still within the domain of the earth tattva.

The very bottom of the metaphysical hierarchy already contains an incalculable multiplicity of cosmic worlds.

Now, if you’re sitting there thinking:

“Bloody hell, that’s quite big.”

I’ve got some devastating news.

We’re about to go up one level.

And the next principle is ten times more extensive.

5. The cosmic Russian dolls
Right.

We’ve established the earth principle.

Pṛthivī.

Absolutely enormous.

Now comes:

Jala-tattva.

Water.

And the domain of water is ten times more extensive than that of earth.

Now don’t imagine a gigantic ocean physically surrounding the entire observable universe.

We’re talking about the hierarchy of principles and their corresponding domains of manifestation.

Next comes:

Tejas.

Fire.

Ten times more extensive again.

Then:

Vāyu.

Air.

Again, tenfold.

Then:

Ākāśa.

Space or ether.

And the sequence continues upward through the subtler principles.

Now within this hierarchy, we have five tanmātras.

The subtle potentials corresponding to sound, touch, form, taste and smell.

And eleven indriyas.

Five powers of perception.

Five powers of action.

And the mind.

All contained within the domain of:

Ahaṃkāra.

The I-maker.

Now this is interesting.

Because ahaṃkāra isn’t simply vanity.

It’s the principle through which experience becomes appropriated in the form:

“I.”

“I see.”

“I hear.”

“I taste.”

“I am doing this.”

And the differentiated faculties and subtle sensory potentials belong within its domain.

Now, above ahaṃkāra comes:

Buddhi.

Intellect.

And the domain of buddhi is one hundred times that of ahaṃkāra.

Then:

Prakṛti.

Primordial nature.

One thousand times that of buddhi.

And this establishes the domain called:

Prakṛtyaṇḍa.

The egg of Prakṛti.

Now here’s another lovely detail.

Just as there are innumerable Brahmāṇḍas, there are innumerable Prakṛtyaṇḍas.

So we’ve got countless cosmic eggs.

And countless greater eggs belonging to primordial nature.

And the larger principle encompasses the lower domains.

But don’t mistake these for containers stacked inside a cupboard.

The language of surrounding and pervading refers to a metaphysical hierarchy.

Each higher principle has a more comprehensive domain than the ones beneath it.

And now we reach an important boundary.

Because we’re about to leave Prakṛti behind and enter the principles responsible for limited individuality.

6. How Śiva becomes a tiny little individual
Now we come to:

Puruṣa-tattva.

The individual experiencer.

Number twelve.

And its domain is ten thousand times that of Prakṛti.

Now, in ordinary Sāṃkhya philosophy, puruṣa is treated as a conscious principle distinct from prakṛti.

But within our Trika framework, puruṣa remains a limited manifestation of the supreme consciousness.

The individual subject.

The one who experiences itself as separate.

And now we’re going to examine the principles that structure this limited condition.

First:

Niyati.

Restriction or determination.

Its domain is one hundred thousand times that of puruṣa.

Now, the Sanskrit niyati carries the sense of constraint, order or necessity.

In the larger Trika system, it refers to the principle through which the unrestricted freedom of consciousness appears subject to particular conditions and relations.

You’re not simply capable of doing anything anywhere.

You act in particular circumstances.

Then:

Rāga.

Attachment.

Or the sense of incompleteness that gives rise to wanting.

Then:

Aśuddhavidyā.

Limited knowledge.

You know this.

You don’t know that.

You apprehend some things and remain ignorant of others.

Then:

Kāla.

Time.

You’re restricted to temporal succession.

Then:

Kalā.

Limited agency.

You possess particular powers of action rather than unrestricted creative freedom.

Now, don’t confuse the two.

Kāla with a long first vowel means time.

Kalā with a long final vowel here means a limited portion or capacity of action.

Very important distinction.

And these limiting principles are assigned progressively greater domains, with a millionfold increase at each successive level beyond niyati up to kalā.

Now someone might object:

“Abhinava, how can limitation be more extensive than the limited individual?”

Good question.

Because you’re still thinking about these things like physical boxes.

A universal principle of limitation can encompass innumerable limited individuals.

The principle is more comprehensive than any particular instance of its operation.

And now we reach the source of this differentiated condition.

Māyā.

Number six.

Its domain is ten million times that of kalā.

And here we have:

Māyāṇḍa.

The egg of Māyā.

Now, within this metaphysical framework, Māyā doesn’t simply mean that the world is a stupid illusion you ought to ignore.

It’s the power through which consciousness manifests differentiated, limited experience.

The source of the division between the restricted knower and the world apparently standing apart from it.

And everything we’ve encountered so far — the individual experiencer, its limitations and the lower material principles — falls within this greater domain.

But here’s the wonderful part.

Māyā is not the highest reality.

Even the power of limitation is itself contained within something more fundamental.

And now we enter the pure tattvas.

7. Above Māyā: the divine principles
Right.

We’re leaving the limited domain behind.

First:

Śuddhavidyā.

Pure knowledge.

And its domain is one hundred million times that of Māyā.

Then:

Īśvara-tattva.

One billion times more extensive than Śuddhavidyā.

Then:

Sadāśiva.

Also called Sādākhya.

Ten billion times more extensive than Īśvara.

And then:

Śakti-tattva.

One hundred billion times more extensive than Sadāśiva.

Now, the entire domain extending through these pure principles up to Śakti is called:

Śaktyaṇḍa.

The egg of Śakti.

Now, consider the scale.

We began with a cosmic egg containing innumerable worlds.

Then we entered greater domains.

Each one more comprehensive than the last.

And now the power of Śakti encompasses the entire hierarchy beneath her.

Which is why she’s called:

Vyāpinī.

The all-pervading one.

From the root vyāp, to pervade or extend throughout.

Because Śakti permeates the entire cosmic path.

Viśvam adhvānam.

Both inwardly and outwardly.

Antar-bahir.

Inside and outside.

She’s not a little goddess occupying one particular chamber of the universe.

She pervades the whole manifestation.

And now comes one of the most important principles in the chapter.

Listen carefully.

8. The higher encompasses the lower
Abhinavagupta gives us a rule.

उत्तरं व्यापकं पूर्वं व्याप्यम्
