The succeeding, higher principle pervades; the preceding, lower principle is pervaded.

Now this is absolutely fundamental.

Imagine you’ve been thinking of the hierarchy like a ladder.

Earth at the bottom.

Water above it.

Fire above that.

And so on until Śiva.

And you might imagine that each one is simply sitting on top of the previous one.

Like floors in a gigantic apartment building.

But that’s not quite right.

The higher principle pervades the lower.

So when you move upward through the hierarchy, you’re not merely travelling farther away.

You’re moving toward increasingly comprehensive principles of manifestation.

The lower has its being within the higher.

And at the summit of the entire sequence?

Śiva-tattva.

And here’s where the numerical measurements finally give up.

Because Śiva is:

Aparimeya.

Immeasurable.

Sarvādhvottīrṇa.

Transcending every cosmic path.

And:

Sarvādhvavyāpaka.

Pervading every cosmic path.

Both.

Again.

Just like the beginning of the chapter.

Beyond everything.

And present throughout everything.

Now, why is this so significant?

Because you’ve spent the entire chapter ascending through increasingly vast principles.

And perhaps you imagined that eventually we’d reach the biggest possible container.

Some final, truly gigantic cosmic sphere.

But no.

Śiva is not merely the biggest object in existence.

He’s the consciousness that transcends and pervades the entire hierarchy of objects and their principles.

Which means that the cosmic hierarchy, however vast, doesn’t exhaust his nature.

And now Abhinavagupta turns to something rather peculiar.

Because apparently all these metaphysical worlds have divine rulers.

And those rulers maintain an interesting relationship with places on earth.

9. The landlords of the universe
Right.

Between the tattvas are numerous worlds.

Bhuvana.

And those worlds possess presiding lords.

Bhuvaneśvara.

Lords of worlds.

Now, Abhinavagupta says these divine rulers also have a presence within the earthly domain.

And here’s the part that’ll get your attention.

People who die in particular sacred places may, according to this tradition, receive passage to the worlds associated with their presiding deities.

The rulers grant them the corresponding destination.

Gati.

And through the process of initiation:

Dīkṣā.

They may be progressively directed toward higher realms.

Now, that’s a significant claim.

We’re not merely talking about geography.

We’re talking about a sacred map in which particular terrestrial places correspond to levels of cosmic existence.

A temple isn’t just a building.

Within this system, it may be an earthly locus of a power or ruler belonging to a particular cosmic domain.

And death in such a place is held to possess soteriological significance.

Now, before you start planning your next holiday around dying in the correct temple, remember that this is the traditional tantric account of postmortem destiny.

It’s not an empirically established travel system.

But it does reveal something important about Abhinavagupta’s worldview.

Cosmic geography, sacred geography, initiation and liberation are connected.

And now he’s going to name the worlds.

All of them in this particular enumeration.

Here comes the divine register.

Get comfortable.

10. Nivṛtti-kalā — the sixteen worlds of the earth principle
First, we’ve got the lowest great division.

Nivṛtti-kalā.

Nivṛtti can mean withdrawal or turning back.

And in this scheme, Nivṛtti comprises sixteen worlds associated with Pṛthivī-tattva.

Sixteen.

Now, five are within the cosmic egg.

