Ananta.

Kapālī.

Vahni.

Anta.

Nirṛti.

Bala.

Laghu.

Nidhipati.

Vidyādhipa.

Śambhu.

And Vīrabhadra.

Now that’s quite an assembly.

You’ve got divine rulers associated with the lower cosmic regions, the directions, and the wider field of the earth principle.

And remember what I said earlier about the hundred Rudras outside the Brahmāṇḍa.

Don’t confuse that general reference with this particular sixteen-world enumeration.

We’re now giving a specific account of the worlds and presiding powers assigned to the earth principle.

Sixteen domains.

All within:

Nivṛtti-kalā.

Now, the important point isn’t that you need to memorise sixteen obscure divine names before breakfast.

It’s that even the lowest tattva contains an internally differentiated collection of worlds.

The universe is not merely one homogeneous physical place.

It’s an entire hierarchy of possible experiences and their corresponding divine powers.

Now we ascend.

And the next division has considerably more worlds.

11. Pratiṣṭhā-kalā — fifty-six worlds
Next comes:

Pratiṣṭhā.

Establishment.

Foundation.

And this division extends from the water principle up to Prakṛti.

How many worlds?

