Bhairava.

Kedāra.

Mahākāla.

Madhya.

Āmra.

Jalpa.

Śrīśaila.

Hariścandra.

These are called the Guhyas.

The secret ones.

Now, imagine being told that Bhairava, Kedāra, Mahākāla and Śrīśaila all belong to one division of a cosmic hierarchy associated with fire.

It’s an extraordinary integration of sacred names.

And remember: the classification is into metaphysical domains, not simply geographical locations on an ordinary map.

Right.

Eight more.

The eight very secret worlds of air
Now we have the Atiguhyas.

The extremely secret ones.

Bhīma.

Indra.

Aṭṭa.

Vimala.

Kanakhala.

Nākhala.

Kurusthiti.

Gaya.

And this grouping is associated with air, together with the subtle sensory elements and the faculties.

Now some of these names may sound familiar.

Gaya, for instance.

And yes, names associated with real sacred geography also appear in this cosmological tradition.

But the point here is their role within the hierarchy of worlds.

Not that you can physically walk from one metaphysical tattva into another by purchasing a train ticket.

That would make Indian Railways considerably more impressive than it already is.

The eight worlds of ether
Next:

Ākāśa.

Space.

And its eight lords are:

Sthāṇu.

Suvarṇa.

Bhadra.

Gokarṇaka.

Mahālayaka.

Avimukta.

Rudrakoṭi.

Vastrāpada.

Now, Avimukta is a particularly evocative name.

Literally associated with what is not abandoned or released.

And in Śaiva sacred geography it has a powerful connection with Vārāṇasī.

Imagine that.

The sacred city associated with Śiva appears within a wider symbolic geography of cosmic manifestation.

Now the relationship between earthly sacred places and the higher worlds becomes a little clearer.

The geography of Śaivism is integrated into the cosmology of Śaivism.

But we’re not finished.

The eight worlds of ahaṃkāra
Next, the I-maker.

Ahaṃkāra.

And the eight are:

Sthūla.

Sthūleśa.

Śaṅkuśruti.

Kāla.

Maṇḍalabhṛt.

Mākoṭa.

Aṇḍa.

Chagalāṇḍa.

Now, Abhinavagupta inserts a small qualification here.

Some authorities place the subtle elements and sense faculties within the domain of ahaṃkāra.

So there’s a difference of classification.

Now, that might not seem especially exciting.

But I rather like it.

Because he’s not pretending that every authoritative source organises every detail identically.

He’s acknowledging a variant account.

And he’s doing so inside an otherwise extremely confident exposition of the universe.

Very good.

Now onward.

The eight worlds of buddhi
Within buddhi, the intellect, are eight divine classes.

Deva-yonayaḥ.

Eight classes or lineages of divine beings.

He doesn’t enumerate their individual names here.

And we’re not going to invent them for dramatic effect.

Then, in Prakṛti:

The eight yogas of primordial nature
There are eight associated states or domains of yoga.

Beginning with:

Akṛta.

Again, he gives the group and its starting name rather than spelling out all eight individually.

And with that, we arrive at the total.

Seven groups of eight

56
All contained within Pratiṣṭhā-kalā, extending from water through Prakṛti.

Now, think about how much we’ve covered.

Earth.

Water.

Fire.

Air.

Ether.

Sensory principles.

Ego.

Intellect.

Primordial nature.

And all their respective divine worlds.

But here’s the thing.

We’re still below the limited individual and the higher principles of māyā.

So we haven’t reached the end of differentiation.

Next comes another major division.

12. Vidyā-kalā — twenty-eight worlds
Right.

Vidyā-kalā.

The division associated with knowledge.

And this extends from Puruṣa to Māyā.

Now we’ve already discussed how these principles are responsible for the limited individual condition.

So the next collection of worlds belongs to this domain.

And Abhinavagupta gives us another enumeration.

First, in Puruṣa:

Eleven Rudras.

Beginning with:

Vāma.

Then two worlds each in three limiting principles:

Vidyā.

Kalā.

Niyati.

That’s six more.

Then, in Kāla, the principle of time:

Three worlds.

Then in Māyā:

Eight worlds.

Now let’s add those up.

Eleven.

Plus six.

Plus three.

Plus eight.

Twenty-eight.

And these comprise Vidyā-kalā.

Now, here’s what I want you to notice.

The worlds are grouped according to the principles of limited existence.

The individual experiencer.

Limited knowledge.

Limited action.

Determination.

Time.

And the greater differentiating power of māyā.

This is not a random list of heavenly addresses.

The classification reflects the structure of manifestation.

The powers governing different cosmic realms correspond to the principles through which experience is conditioned.

And now we’re about to leave Māyā behind.

Which means the next division is going to be rather different.

13. Śānti-kalā — eighteen worlds of the pure path
Now we enter:

Śānti.

Peace.

Pacification.

The division associated here with the pure principles above Māyā.

And Abhinavagupta gives us three groups.

First:

Śuddhavidyā.

Five worlds.

Then:

Īśvara-tattva.

Eight worlds.

Associated with the eight:

Vidyeśvaras.

Lords of knowledge.

And then:

Sadāśiva.

Five worlds.

So:

Five.

Eight.

Five.

Eighteen.

And that’s:

Śānti-kalā.

Now, note where we are.

We’ve moved from the densely differentiated material domains through the limited individual and into the pure divine principles.

And the hierarchy is becoming increasingly comprehensive.

But remember the earlier statement.

Even the highest enumerated worlds are pervaded by Śiva.

And Śiva himself is immeasurable.

So the point isn’t to find the highest conceivable world and take up permanent residence there.

You’re still thinking in terms of places.

The supreme recognition concerns the conscious reality underlying the very possibility of worlds.

Now we’ve finished the enumeration.

Let’s look at the complete arrangement.

