Right then.

Last chapter we discussed the spatial universe.

Deśādhva.

We started with a little patch of earth, discovered innumerable cosmic eggs, travelled through 118 divine worlds, ascended the hierarchy of reality, and eventually arrived at the immeasurable consciousness of Śiva.

Absolutely magnificent.

But there’s a slight problem.

We’ve now got this enormous cosmic hierarchy.

Thirty-six tattvas.

All these principles.

Earth.

Water.

Fire.

Mind.

Intellect.

Māyā.

Divine knowledge.

Śakti.

Śiva.

And you’re probably wondering:

“Abhinava, mate, what exactly ARE these things? Are they substances? Categories? Cosmic departments? Who’s making all of this happen?”

Excellent question.

Because today we’re going to investigate the essential nature of the tattvas.

Tattvasvarūpa.

And more importantly, how they arise.

Now, if you’re expecting a straightforward explanation of how one thing produces another, I’ve got bad news.

Abhinavagupta is about to challenge your entire understanding of cause and effect.

And then, once he’s finished dismantling causation, he’s going to explain exactly how Śiva becomes an individual with limited knowledge, attachment, a body, five senses and a rather unfortunate habit of thinking he’s separate from everything.

Let’s begin.

1. What actually makes something a tattva?
Right.

Remember all those worlds we discussed?

Bhuvana.

Countless divine realms.

Countless experiencers.

Countless objects of experience.

Bhoktṛ.

The enjoyer.

Bhogya.

That which is enjoyed or experienced.

Now look at this enormous collection of worlds.

What do they all have in common?

Well, ultimately there’s something present throughout the entire collection.

A single great luminosity.

Mahāprakāśa.

The great light of consciousness.

And because this luminosity pervades absolutely everything, we call it:

Mahāsāmānya.

The great universal.

And what is this universal reality?

Paramaśiva.

The supreme Śiva.

Now, don’t imagine this is a universal property like all chairs having something in common.

We’re not merely classifying objects.

We’re speaking of the conscious reality through which all these worlds and their distinctions appear.

But suppose something pervades only a particular group of things.

Not absolutely everything.

Just some particular class of manifestations.

Well, that is what we’re calling a:

Tattva.

A principle of reality.

For example:

Pṛthivī.

Earth.

Now, the earth tattva isn’t merely that handful of dirt you picked up in the garden.

It’s the principle characterised by qualities such as solidity, hardness and grossness.

And this principle pervades the entire range of worlds associated with it, from Kālagni to Vīrabhadra.

So we’ve got two levels.

The universal luminosity pervading absolutely everything.

And particular principles pervading particular ranges of manifestation.

Right.

Now here’s the question.

How does one tattva produce another?

Ah.

Now we’re getting into trouble.

2. There are two kinds of causation
You probably think you already understand causation.

You plant a seed.

A plant grows.

You heat water.

It boils.

You knock over your cup.

Tea everywhere.

And you conclude:

“Well, the first thing caused the second.”

Perfectly sensible.

But Abhinavagupta says we need to distinguish two kinds of causation.

First:

Pāramārthika-kāryakāraṇabhāva.

Causation in the ultimate sense.

Second:

Kalpita-kāryakāraṇabhāva.

Constructed or conventionally established causation.

Now, this isn’t quite the same as saying:

“One is real and the other is just fake.”

Because the conventional sequence is itself manifested through divine freedom.

But there is a fundamental distinction.

Let’s begin with ultimate causation.

And here’s the extraordinary teaching.

The supreme Lord manifests himself as the entire hierarchy of reality.

From Śiva-tattva right down to earth.

Śivādi-dharānta.

Everything.

And these manifested forms are both distinct in appearance from his essential nature and resting within that very nature.

Now listen carefully.

Śiva doesn’t take some foreign material and start assembling the universe.

There’s no foreign material.

Nothing independently existing outside him.

He manifests himself through his own freedom.

Svātantrya.

And because he’s inherently the agent, kartṛ, this manifestation is his own activity.

So the ultimate causal relationship is this:

Free consciousness manifests itself as everything, while everything remains grounded in free consciousness.

That’s the real causal foundation.

Now.

What about ordinary causation?

The seed and the sprout?

The fire and the smoke?

The potter and the pot?

Ah.

That brings us to a rather interesting argument.

3. A seed doesn’t necessarily need to make a sprout
Right.

Suppose I plant a seed.

A sprout appears.

You say:

“The seed caused the sprout.”

Very good.

But what exactly establishes that particular causal relationship?

Why must a sprout follow a seed?

Why must this cause precede that effect?

Abhinavagupta says the fixed ordering of cause and effect is manifested through:

Niyati.

The power of determination.

And niyati itself operates through the will of the supreme Lord.

Parameśecchā.

So the regularities of conventional causation are not independent laws standing above Śiva.

They’re expressions of his freedom.

Now, he gives a rather provocative example.

A sprout might arise through the will of a yogin.

Yogīcchā.

And in ordinary experience a sprout arises from a seed.

But in dreams, other arrangements of things can appear.

A pot, for example, might appear without the ordinary waking sequence of clay, wheel and potter.

Now, Abhinavagupta isn’t claiming that ordinary plants routinely sprout without seeds.

He’s challenging the idea that one particular causal sequence is metaphysically necessary in every conceivable manifestation.

Within the ordinary world, the sequence is established.

But that sequence itself depends upon divine freedom.

And the ultimate causal foundation remains the same, whether the appearance follows an ordinary sequence or an extraordinary one.

Śiva’s creative agency doesn’t disappear just because a particular event has an apparent intermediate cause.

Now, let’s take an even more familiar example.

A pot.

And its potter.

4. The potter thinks he’s the one making the pot
Imagine a potter.

Lovely fellow.

He’s got his clay.

His wheel.

His tools.

His little workshop.

And he’s making a pot.

Very impressive.

You ask:

“Who made this?”

And he says:

“I did.”

Fair enough.

But Abhinavagupta has a question.

What exactly does the potter contribute that is independent of the whole universe?

His body?

That’s part of the universe.

His hands?

Part of the universe.

The clay?

Part of the universe.

The wheel?

Also part of the universe.

His knowledge of pottery?

That’s an expression of consciousness.

His intentions?

Also consciousness.

Even the circumstances that brought him into the workshop are part of the larger arrangement.

So where exactly are we going to locate this completely independent potter?

Nowhere.

And what’s especially funny is that the potter’s conviction:

“I am the maker of this pot”

is itself something manifested through the supreme Lord.

The potter’s agency is real within the conventional arrangement.

But it isn’t ultimately independent.

The potter’s consciousness isn’t something greater than or separate from supreme consciousness.

And his body belongs to the totality of manifested circumstances.

So Abhinavagupta concludes that, ultimately, the maker of the pot is:

Parameśvara.

The supreme Lord.

Not because the human potter is irrelevant.

But because the potter, his knowledge, the materials and the entire causal arrangement are expressions of one cosmic agency.

And now he makes an important refinement.

The immediate cause of an ordinary effect is not some isolated little object.

It’s the entire relevant complex of conditions.

Sāmagrī.

The causal complex.

Imagine making a pot.

You’ve got clay.

Water.

A wheel.

Heat.

A particular skill.

A particular environment.

Change the arrangement sufficiently, and the result changes.

Abhinavagupta gives the example of Mount Meru.

A pot of precisely the same kind wouldn’t arise there merely because a potter and some clay happened to be present.

The complete circumstances matter.

And because every causal complex exists within the totality of consciousness, even this more sophisticated account of causation ultimately supports the agency of the supreme Lord.

Now consider something else.

Different scriptures describe different causal relationships between the tattvas.

One says this principle emerges from that.

Another arranges the sequence differently.

Should we panic?

Absolutely not.

Because at the conventional level, different causal sequences can be manifested.

Abhinavagupta even invokes traditional examples of scorpions arising from cow dung, worms, the will of yogins, mantras or medicinal substances.

Rather ambitious biological claims.

We’re not endorsing medieval zoology here.

We’re understanding the point he’s making.

A particular causal sequence is not the ultimate source of reality.

The ultimate source is divine freedom.

And now that we’ve established this, Abhinavagupta says:

Right.

Let’s explain the sequence according to our own Tantra.

And this is where the entire thirty-six-tattva system starts unfolding.

5. The five pure tattvas — Śiva’s five powers
Remember what I explained in Chapter One?

Parameśvara possesses five principal powers.

Cit.

Ānanda.

Icchā.

Jñāna.

Kriyā.

Consciousness.

Bliss.

Will.

Knowledge.

Action.

Now, these are not five separate substances.

They’re powers of one supreme consciousness.

But here’s the crucial thing.

Śiva can make any one of these powers predominant.

And depending upon which power predominates, we describe a different tattva.

First:

Śiva-tattva — Cit-śakti
When the power of consciousness predominates, we speak of:

Śiva-tattva.

Pure luminosity.

Cit.

The power of awareness itself.

Now, don’t imagine that the other four powers have disappeared.

They’re still present.

But consciousness is the dominant aspect.

Second:

Śakti-tattva — Ānanda-śakti
When bliss predominates:

Śakti-tattva.

The fullness of delight.

Ānanda.

Not excitement about getting something you wanted.

Not pleasure dependent upon an external object.

The inherent fullness of unrestricted consciousness.

Third:

Sadāśiva-tattva — Icchā-śakti
Now we have the predominance of will.

Sadāśiva.

Icchā.

And Abhinavagupta gives an interesting reason.

Will represents a balanced acceptance of the powers of knowledge and action.

The intention preceding their more explicit differentiation.

Consciousness is oriented toward manifestation, but knowledge and action have not yet predominated separately.

Fourth:

Īśvara-tattva — Jñāna-śakti
Now knowledge predominates.

Īśvara.

Jñāna.

The power of explicit apprehension.

And fifth:

Śuddhavidyā-tattva — Kriyā-śakti
Finally:

Śuddhavidyā.

Pure knowledge, but here specifically associated with the predominance of:

Kriyā-śakti.

The power of action.

The unfolding of differentiated manifestation within the pure divine domain.

THE FIVE PURE TATTVAS

1

Śiva

Cit — consciousness

2

Śakti

Ānanda — bliss

3

Sadāśiva

Icchā — will

4

Īśvara

Jñāna — knowledge

5

Śuddhavidyā

Kriyā — action

Now notice something important.

It’s the same Śiva throughout.

We’re not moving between five competing cosmic beings.

We’re describing the predominance of five powers belonging to one free consciousness.

And each pure tattva possesses its presiding lord.

Śiva.

Śakti.

Sadāśiva.

Īśvara.

And Ananta.

There are also five corresponding classes of divine beings:

Śāmbhavas.

Śāktas.

Mantramaheśvaras.

Mantreśvaras.

Mantras.

And together they constitute:

Śuddhādhva.

The pure cosmic path.

Now here’s the crucial distinction.

Throughout this pure domain, Śiva is directly the creator.

But something is about to happen.

We’re going to leave the pure path.

And enter:

Aśuddhādhva.

The impure or differentiated path.

And to understand why that happens, we need to investigate something rather uncomfortable.

Because apparently the infinite Lord has developed a peculiar fascination with being incomplete.

6. The great cosmic problem: Śiva wants to experience being incomplete
Right.

Now we enter the impure path.

And the presiding creator here is a divine being called:

Aghoreśa.

Also known as:

Ananta.

He operates according to the will of Īśvara.

And what is he creating?

A world of differentiated experience for limited subjects.

Aṇus.

The contracted individuals.

Now here’s the question.

Why do these individuals require such a world?

Because they’re disturbed by a peculiar longing for experience.

Bhoga-lolikā.

An eagerness to enjoy or experience something.

And Abhinavagupta explains that this longing involves:

Apūrṇammanyatā.

The sense of oneself as incomplete.

Now let’s look at that word.

Apūrṇa — incomplete, not full.

Manyatā — considering or regarding something in a particular way.

So:

Considering oneself incomplete.

That’s the problem.

Not necessarily being metaphysically incomplete.

But experiencing oneself as though something were missing.

Imagine infinite consciousness.

Completely free.

Entirely full.

Nothing outside it.

Nothing it needs to acquire.

And somehow it comes to experience:

“Hmm.”

“Something’s missing.”

“Perhaps I ought to get something.”

Absolutely extraordinary.

What the bloody hell could infinite consciousness be missing?

Nothing.

But that sense of deficiency is the beginning of the contracted individual’s desire for experience.

Now, Abhinavagupta makes a subtle distinction.

This first longing isn’t yet a particular action directed toward a particular object.

It’s simply an urge.

Abhilāṣa-mātra.

Bare desire.

An orientation toward a possible future limitation.

And that fundamental condition is connected with:

Mala.

Impurity.

But not impurity in the sense that you’ve got dirt on your soul.

And not yet another tattva that we’ve forgotten to count.

Mala here is the concealment of fullness.

The condition in which consciousness appears diminished.

Now, distinguish this from:

Rāga-tattva.

Attachment.

Rāga is already a more particular form of desire, conditioned by activity.

You aren’t merely experiencing some vague sense of incompleteness.

You’re now wanting something.

“I want that.”

“I want to do this.”

“I need to experience that.”

The vague sense of deficiency has become directed toward particular possibilities.

And karma — activity — is another matter again.

We’ll explain the relationships properly when we reach the lower tattvas.

But here’s the extraordinary claim.

Mala is not some foreign substance that has invaded Śiva.

It arises through the supreme Lord’s own will to conceal his nature.

Svātma-pracchādana-icchā.

The desire to conceal himself.

Now, that’s going to upset anyone who imagines bondage must have been inflicted upon consciousness by something outside it.

There’s nothing outside Śiva capable of imprisoning him.

The contraction itself belongs to his freedom.

But we need to distinguish the conditions of different limited beings.

7. Some souls are nearly free; others are still absolutely tangled
Now Abhinavagupta introduces two classes of limited subject.

First:

Vijñānākala.

A being whose condition of impurity is moving toward dissolution.

The fundamental limitation hasn’t necessarily been completely removed.

But it is no longer nourishing fresh karmic involvement in the same way.

Then:

Pralayākala.

A being in a condition associated with cosmic dissolution.

Here mala remains active or capable of further expansion.

And when strengthened by it, karma becomes a cause of the extraordinary diversity of saṃsāric experiences.

Pleasure.

Pain.

Different births.

Different circumstances.

All the varied conditions of limited existence.

Now, because these beings retain tendencies toward experience, the divine creator manifests the appropriate world.

The world is the field in which their dispositions and karmic conditions can unfold.

But there’s another important point.

Mala itself doesn’t suddenly wake up and start manufacturing karma.

It’s inert.

Jaḍa.

It has no independent power of action.

So what activates this entire process?

Īśvarecchā.

The will of the Lord.

Again.

Abhinavagupta keeps bringing us back to the same principle.

No matter how complicated the cosmic machinery becomes, the ultimate power of manifestation belongs to supreme consciousness.

And now we’re going to define the aṇu itself.

8. The individual has a rather peculiar double nature
What exactly is an individual soul?

An aṇu?

Abhinavagupta says it appears in two aspects.

Cit.

Conscious.

And:

Acit.

Nonconscious.

Now this is a fascinating distinction.

The conscious aspect is:

Aiśvarya.

Lordship.

The divine nature.

The power belonging to consciousness itself.

And the nonconscious aspect?

That is associated with mala.

Limitation.

So the individual isn’t some entirely different substance from Śiva.

Its conscious reality is divine.

But it appears accompanied by a condition of insentience or limitation.

And because Śiva freely manifests such a condition, there arises a principle capable of serving as the material basis of further differentiated manifestations.

And that principle is:

Māyā-tattva.

Now, be very careful.

Because Abhinavagupta is about to distinguish two things that have the same name.

First, there’s Māyā as a tattva.

An insentient, pervasive causal principle.

The material basis, upādāna-kāraṇa, of the lower manifestation.

And then there’s Māyā as Śiva’s power.

The divine Śakti that manifests this tattva.

Those are not identical in function.

The first is an insentient principle within manifestation.

The second is the conscious divine power through which that principle appears.

Do you see why that matters?

Because otherwise you’d hear that Māyā is inert and immediately conclude that Śiva’s power is somehow unconscious.

Absolutely not.

Māyā-tattva is a manifestation of the conscious power, not the ultimate nature of that power.

And Abhinavagupta says a similar twofold understanding should be applied throughout the lower tattvas, from kalā down to earth.

Each has its particular manifested character, while its reality is grounded in Śiva’s creative power.

Now, at this point he invokes the teaching of his own gurus.

And it’s rather provocative.

9. Even things imagined in consciousness have a mode of manifestation
His teachers give an argument concerning things that appear in conceptual imagination.

Saṃkalpa.

Take a pot.

You can imagine a pot.

And a pot can also appear in an externally differentiated, perceptible form.

The teachers extend this reasoning to subtle principles such as Māyā, kalā, prakṛti and buddhi.

Even to the classical example of an impossible thing:

A sky-flower.

Kha-puṣpa.

Now, this isn’t a sensible excuse to claim that every imaginary object can be found physically sitting in the garden.

The argument concerns different modes of appearance and the relation between what is contemplated and what can be manifested.

And here’s the practical implication.

A yogin who attains direct awareness of these subtle principles may be considered accomplished.

Siddha.

Even if their object is Māyā, kalā, prakṛti or buddhi rather than a gross physical thing.

Because direct contemplative access to these principles has significance within this cosmology.

Right.

Now we’ve established the material basis of manifestation.

Māyā.

And from Māyā, the world unfolds.

Viśvaprasava.

The birth of the universe.

But before we go any further, there’s another question.

Does all of this happen in an actual chronological sequence?

10. Does Śiva create everything one thing at a time?
Right.

Here’s the difficulty.

We’re about to say:

Māyā produces kalā.

Kalā produces limited knowledge.

Then attachment.

Time.

Determination.

And so forth.

But didn’t we establish earlier that supreme consciousness isn’t constrained by time?

How can the ultimate creative act require one thing to exist before another?

Excellent.

Now you’re paying attention.

Abhinavagupta says that in its ultimate nature, creation is:

Akrama.

Nonsequential.

But it can nevertheless appear as:

Krama.

Sequential.

And that’s the important distinction.

The hierarchy is an intelligible ordering of manifestation.

It doesn’t mean Śiva has to wait for one cosmic factory to finish manufacturing a tattva before he can start the next production line.

Moreover, different individuals possess different limiting conditions.

The collection of principles beginning with kalā can differ from one individual to another.

Yet through divine will, these powers may also be brought into a shared arrangement.

Like people participating in one gathering.

Different individual experiences, but a common manifested circumstance.

Now, here’s something particularly important.

The same limiting powers can function differently depending upon their orientation.

If they operate in a way that supports recognition of Parameśvara, they are described as pure.

If they reinforce the contracted experience of saṃsāra, they are impure.

And what brings about the transformative orientation?

Śaktipāta.

The descent of divine power.

We’ll discuss that properly later.

For now, understand that the issue isn’t simply whether you possess particular faculties.

It’s whether those faculties reinforce separateness or serve recognition.

And now, at last, we can descend through the specific limiting principles.

This is where Śiva gets his little individual identity.

11. Kalā — from unlimited agency to “I can do a few things”
First:

Kalā-tattva.

The principle of limited agency.

Now remember the absolute power of Śiva.

Kriyā-śakti.

Unrestricted creative action.

No limitation.

Nothing outside its capacity.

But when consciousness assumes the contracted condition of individuality, its agency appears restricted.

And kalā provides the limited individual with:

Kiṃcit-kartṛtva.

The capacity to do something.

Let’s break that down.

Kiṃcit — something, a little, a particular amount.

Kartṛtva — agency, the condition of being an agent.

So:

A little bit of agency.

Quite a downgrade.

Imagine going from:

“I freely manifest the entire universe.”

To:

“I reckon I can make a sandwich.”

And even that’s assuming you’ve got bread.

Now, how does kalā arise?

Through the conjunction of Māyā and the contracted individual.

And Abhinavagupta makes a crucial distinction.

The individual consciousness isn’t intrinsically altered.

Rather, Māyā undergoes the modification through which limited agency appears.

The conscious principle remains, in its essence, immutable.

But its condition of manifestation is now different.

The result is such intimate conjunction between the individual and kalā that their distinction becomes exceedingly difficult to perceive.

You don’t ordinarily think:

“Ah, here is pure awareness, and here is the limiting principle through which agency appears.”

You simply say:

“I am doing this.”

And take that limited agency to be the entirety of yourself.

Now this brings us to a remarkable discussion of liberation.

12. There are different depths of liberation
Abhinavagupta explains that, through the grace of a divine lord and the exhaustion of karma, different discriminations can arise.

Viveka.

Discernment.

First, you may distinguish the individual consciousness from Māyā.

Māyā-puruṣa-viveka.

This permits the condition of the Vijñānākala, above Māyā.

Or you may distinguish consciousness from kalā.

Kalā-puṃ-viveka.

And rest above that limiting principle.

Or distinguish puruṣa from prakṛti.

Prakṛti-puruṣa-viveka.

And no longer wander within the domain governed by primordial nature.

Now here’s the really important distinction.

Even these liberations are understood according to the particular limitation that has been overcome.

If mala itself is distinguished from puruṣa, there’s an attainment comparable to Śiva.

Śiva-samānatva.

But when the fullness of puruṣa is directly recognised?

Puruṣa-pūrṇatā-dṛṣṭi.

Then:

Śivatvam eva.

Śiva-hood itself.

Not merely escape from one particular realm.

Not merely freedom from some limited condition.

The recognition of fullness.

And notice the recurring principle.

Liberation isn’t about running higher and higher up the cosmic ladder indefinitely.

It’s about recognition of the supreme nature of consciousness.

Right.

Back to our poor little individual.

We’ve given him limited agency.

But there’s a problem.

You can’t do anything intelligently if you don’t know anything.

So we’re going to need another tattva.

13. Aśuddhavidyā — congratulations, you now know three things
Now comes:

Aśuddhavidyā.

Impure or limited knowledge.

And the important function is:

Kiṃcit-jñatva.

Knowing something.

Not everything.

Just a bit.

So we’ve got:

Kalā — I can do something.

Vidyā — I know something.

And here is where the trouble begins.

Because unlimited consciousness doesn’t know things by standing apart from them as an ignorant subject trying to acquire information.

But the contracted individual does.

Now the individual says:

“I know what that is.”

“I don’t know what that is.”

“I understand this.”

“I’ve never heard of that.”

And the power of limited knowledge makes these restricted cognitions possible.

Now, Abhinavagupta introduces something interesting.

Buddhi.

The intellect.

He says limited vidyā apprehends and distinguishes the appearances reflected within buddhi.

Including experiences such as pleasure.

Sukha.

And other forms of experience.

But buddhi itself has a mixed character.

It contains different qualities.

And it cannot, by itself, perform the required discrimination in the way vidyā does.

So here’s the distinction.

Buddhi provides the field in which the object is reflected.

Vidyā makes a limited discrimination of what’s reflected there.

Imagine looking at a cluttered desk.

Books.

Papers.

Cups.

A half-eaten biscuit.

An enormous collection of nonsense.

Buddhi presents the material of cognition.

Vidyā enables particular determinations.

“That’s my book.”

“That’s tea.”

“That’s a biscuit.”

“That biscuit’s been there for three days.”

Now, the point isn’t that these are physically separate machines inside your skull.

They’re analytically distinct principles of cognition.

And notice the limitation.

You know one thing at a time, or some particular collection of things.

You’re not apprehending the universe through unlimited divine knowledge.

Your own power of knowing appears restricted to particular contents.

Right.

We’ve got agency.

We’ve got knowledge.

What’s missing?

Ah.

Desire.

Because apparently the little bloke is about to become dissatisfied with absolutely everything.

14. Rāga — the cosmic invention of wanting stuff
Now we come to:

Rāga-tattva.

Attachment.

Desire.

The principle through which a limited agent is drawn toward particular objects and experiences.

Remember our earlier distinction?

Mala involves the fundamental appearance of incompleteness.

But rāga is already concerned with particular attractions.

The individual doesn’t merely experience:

“Something’s missing.”

Now it’s:

“I want THAT.”

And Abhinavagupta makes a subtle argument.

Rāga cannot simply be reduced to the absence of detachment.

Avairāgya.

Because people can lack detachment and nevertheless not be attracted to every particular object.

And conversely, someone possessing detachment toward one domain may still exhibit attraction elsewhere.

Even a person who’s eaten their fill may retain an underlying disposition toward food.

So attachment cannot simply be explained as:

“Well, you haven’t become detached yet.”

No.

Rāga is a positive principle of attraction within the structure of limited experience.

Now, imagine you’ve just eaten an enormous meal.

Absolutely stuffed.

You couldn’t possibly manage another bite.

Then someone brings out a magnificent dessert.

Suddenly:

“Well, perhaps there’s a little room.”

There he is.

Rāga.

The little bastard.

But within this philosophical system, it’s much more fundamental than liking pudding.

It’s what makes particular objects appear desirable to a subject who experiences itself as incomplete.

And now we’ve got an individual who can act, can know, and wants things.

What’s missing?

A schedule.

15. Kāla — now you’ve got deadlines
Kāla-tattva.

Time.

Remember Chapter Six?

We spent an extraordinary amount of time discussing time.

Rather ironic.

Now we’re considering it as a limiting principle.

And its function is to determine action through temporal restriction.

The individual isn’t merely thinking:

“I can do something.”

Now the individual experiences:

“I can do this now.”

Or:

“I could do that yesterday.”

“Perhaps I’ll do it tomorrow.”

And suddenly you’ve got the entire nightmare of temporal existence.

Past.

Present.

Future.

Things that are no longer available.

Things that haven’t happened.

Things you wish would last.

Things you wish would end.

And within this system, that limitation is connected with kāla.

Now, supreme consciousness is not intrinsically restricted by temporal succession.

But the limited individual experiences agency through time.

And with that comes the next restriction.

Because even at a particular time, you can’t do absolutely anything.

16. Niyati — you’re not doing that here, mate
Niyati.

Determination.

Restriction.

The principle through which your actions and experiences become bound to particular conditions.

Now imagine:

“I can do something.”

Fine.

“I can do something now.”

Also fine.

But:

“I can do this particular thing, under these particular circumstances.”

That’s niyati.

And it also governs the ordinary relations of cause and effect.

Remember the seed and sprout?

The potter and pot?

The regularity of conventional causation?

We’re back to that.

Niyati is what makes the manifested world appear as an ordered arrangement of particular causal conditions.

So here’s our little individual.

He’s got limited agency.

Limited knowledge.

Attachment.

Time.

And determinate circumstances.

And now he experiences:

“I know this particular thing.”

“I want it.”

“I can act upon it.”

“But only now, and only under these conditions.”

Congratulations.

We’ve successfully turned unrestricted consciousness into someone trying to catch a bus.

Now, Abhinavagupta gives the whole thing a name.

17. The six coverings — Śiva puts on a very restrictive jacket
Kañcuka.

A covering.

A sheath.

Something that envelops.

And Abhinavagupta calls the six principles beginning with Māyā:

Kañcuka-ṣaṭka.

The sixfold covering.

Notice that there are six here because Māyā itself is included.

Sometimes you’ll encounter descriptions of five kañcukas, meaning the five limiting powers below Māyā.

But in this chapter Abhinavagupta explicitly speaks of the sixfold group.

माया
Māyā

Differentiation and concealment

कला
Kalā

Limited agency

विद्या
Vidyā

Limited knowledge

राग
Rāga

Particular attachment

काल
Kāla

Temporal restriction

नियति
Niyati

Determination and causal order

Now, don’t imagine these six coverings are literally pieces of fabric wrapped around your soul.

The metaphor concerns how the unlimited powers of consciousness appear restricted.

And the resulting individual, associated with body and subtle faculties, is called:

Paśu.

The bound being.

Now, this is a particularly important distinction.

The limitation isn’t simply that you’ve got a body.

It’s that consciousness experiences its agency and knowledge in restricted forms and identifies itself with that condition.

Abhinavagupta gives the resulting cognition in a wonderfully compact expression.

The sense is:

“I know this particular thing now, I’m attached to it, and I’m doing something about it.”

That’s the paśu.

The restricted subject.

And if you’ve ever spent an entire afternoon obsessing over one particular problem while forgetting the vastness of existence, you’ll appreciate the description.

But we’re not finished.

Because so far we’ve been describing the limited subject.

Now we need something for that subject to experience.

And that brings us to:

Prakṛti.

18. Prakṛti — the universe produces something for you to experience
Right.

Remember the distinction in the expression:

Kiṃcit-kartṛtva.

Limited agency.

It contains two elements.

Kartṛtva.

Agency.

And:

Kiṃcit.

The particularity, or limitedness, of that agency.

Now we’ve dealt with the agency side through kalā and the associated limiting principles.

But the restricted subject needs an objective field.

Something knowable.

Something experienceable.

And that’s where:

Prakṛti-tattva

enters the picture.

Primordial nature.

Now Prakṛti isn’t yet a fully differentiated collection of chairs, trees, mountains and inconvenient people.

It’s the general condition underlying experienceable objects.

The potential for differentiated enjoyment and suffering.

And its nature is described through three fundamental experiential qualities.

Pleasure.

Sukha.

Pain.

Duḥkha.

And confusion or delusion.

Moha.

Now Abhinavagupta makes an important point.

The limited subject and its experienceable field arise together through the manifestation associated with kalā.

Bhoktṛ-bhogya.

Enjoyer and enjoyed.

Subject and object.

Because what would an enjoyer be without anything to enjoy?

And what would it mean for something to be enjoyed without an enjoyer?

They belong together within the structure of differentiated experience.

Now, don’t imagine that consciousness has actually had to wait for one to be manufactured before making the other.

We’ve already established that creation is ultimately nonsequential.

But it may be described sequentially for the sake of understanding.

And here’s a particularly nice observation.

The order of limited knowledge and attachment can vary.

Sometimes you become attracted to something and then investigate it.

Sometimes you learn about something and only afterward develop an attachment.

Think about that.

You might see a beautiful object and immediately want it.

Then discover what it actually is.

Or you might study something for years and gradually develop an extraordinary love for it.

Different apparent sequences.

Same underlying principles.

So Abhinavagupta tells us not to treat different scriptural enumerations as contradictions merely because they give different orders.

The metaphysical structure doesn’t require one rigid chronology.

Right.

Now let’s see what happens when Prakṛti becomes disturbed.

19. The three guṇas — bliss, misery and absolute confusion
Now we encounter:

Guṇa-tattva.

The domain of the three qualities.

Guṇa.

Now, in Sāṃkhya philosophy these three are fundamental characteristics of Prakṛti.

But Abhinavagupta explicitly distinguishes an agitated condition of the guṇas in his account.

And he makes a point of saying that this separate guṇa-tattva is not recognised in precisely this form by the Sāṃkhyas.

So this is a distinctive moment in his analysis.

Right.

We’ve got three guṇas.

Sattva
Associated with pleasure and illumination.

Sukha.

Prakāśa.

The object shines forth in an agreeable form.

Something becomes manifest and is experienced pleasantly.

Now imagine drinking some magnificent tea.

You’ve brewed it perfectly.

The flavour is clear.

The aroma is delightful.

You’re completely satisfied.

Lovely.

That’s a rough illustration of the illuminating and pleasurable character associated with sattva.

Rajas
Now comes:

Rajas.

Associated here with pain and the oscillation between illumination and nonillumination.

It’s dynamic.

Active.

Restless.

You want something.

You move toward it.

You encounter resistance.

You oscillate between fulfilment and frustration.

And this movement is connected with:

Kriyā.

Activity.

Now imagine making that same cup of tea.

Except you’re absolutely desperate for it.

The kettle’s taking forever.

Someone’s moved your mug.

The water’s gone cold.

You can’t find the tea leaves.

And now you’re having an existential crisis in the kitchen.

Rajas.

Tamas
Finally:

Tamas.

Associated with the absence of illumination.

Prakāśābhāva.

And with:

Moha.

Confusion.

Delusion.

Now, don’t simply equate this with being stupid or sleepy.

The philosophical point is a condition in which manifestation is obscured or concealed.

The object isn’t apprehended clearly.

There’s a kind of darkness or nonillumination.

And these three together constitute the differentiated field of possible experience.

Pleasure.

Pain.

Confusion.

Now, why does this matter?

Because something must happen to Prakṛti for further differentiated manifestation to emerge.

It must become disturbed.

Kṣobha.

Agitation.

The equilibrium of the guṇas becomes active.

And then the further principles emerge.

Now, Abhinavagupta says something important.

This disturbance doesn’t simply happen because Prakṛti is an independently conscious agent.

It occurs under the governance of its presiding lord.

Tattveśa.

Because the whole process must be directed toward a particular experiencer.

And an insentient principle cannot independently determine that relationship.

Once again, the ultimate background is conscious agency.

And with the guṇas disturbed, something arises.

Buddhi.

The intellect.

20. Buddhi — your cosmic mirror
Now we’re going to understand cognition.

Because this is where things get very interesting.

In buddhi, two things are reflected.

First:

The light of the individual subject.

Puṃ-prakāśa.

Second:

The object.

Viṣaya.

So buddhi is a kind of meeting place.

The appearance of the subject and the appearance of the object are both presented within the intellectual principle.

Now imagine looking at a flower.

There’s an apparent knower.

And there’s a flower.

And within buddhi, the flower becomes an object of cognition.

But then something rather unfortunate occurs.

Because the next principle introduces a mistaken identification.

And this is one of the most important parts of the entire chapter.

Ahaṃkāra.

The I-maker.

21. Ahaṃkāra — how the real “I” becomes the fake “I”
Right.

Let’s break down the word.

Aham.

I.

Kāra.

Maker, making.

So:

I-maker.

Now here’s the problem.

The pure consciousness of Śiva possesses genuine self-awareness.

Aham-vimarśa.

The immediate apprehension of I.

Not a conceptual identity.

Not a biography.

Not a name.

Not an ego constructed through experience.

The intrinsic self-awareness of consciousness.

And Abhinavagupta says that this pure I-consciousness is:

Apratiyogi.

Without an opposing counterpart.

It isn’t the limited ego standing against a world of separate objects.

It’s the self-apprehension of unrestricted consciousness.

Svātma-camatkāra.

The wonder or delight of one’s own Self.

Now, what happens in ahaṃkāra?

The light of the subject comes into contact with the object reflected in buddhi.

And the limited individual mistakenly identifies something that is not the Self as the Self.

Anātmani ātmābhimānaḥ.

The attribution of Self to what is not Self.

And Abhinavagupta gives a beautiful classical example.

A piece of mother-of-pearl.

Śukti.

You see it glistening.

And mistake it for silver.

The famous silver illusion.

Now, notice what’s happened.

Something genuinely appears.

There’s a shining object.

But you misidentify it.

You say:

“Silver!”

When in fact it’s mother-of-pearl.

And similarly, the luminous reality of consciousness appears in association with the body, mind and differentiated faculties.

But the individual appropriates these limited appearances as the entirety of the Self.

“I am this body.”

“I am this intellect.”

“I am this particular personality.”

Now, Abhinavagupta makes a rather pointed criticism of Sāṃkhya.

He says Sāṃkhya doesn’t accept the true Self as possessing the same intrinsic I-awareness that his own tradition attributes to consciousness.

And that’s an important difference.

Because for Trika, the pure I is not the problem.

The problem is the contraction of I-consciousness into a limited identity.

The false identification.

Not self-awareness itself.

This is absolutely fundamental.

You’re not trying to destroy the intrinsic aham of consciousness.

You’re recognising that the limited ahaṃkāra is a constructed manifestation of a much deeper self-awareness.

And Abhinavagupta says the very word kāra in ahaṃkāra indicates its constructed character.

It’s been made.

Formed.

Manifested.

Not the unrestricted I-consciousness itself.

Now, this is probably the most important philosophical distinction in the chapter.

Because it’s the difference between:

“I am this limited organism.”

And:

“I am the consciousness in which this organism and its world appear.”

But Abhinavagupta doesn’t stop with the ego.

Because ahaṃkāra has further functions.

And apparently we’re going to need quite a lot of them to build a functioning human being.

22. From ahaṃkāra come your mind and senses
Now Abhinavagupta distinguishes different aspects of ahaṃkāra.

There’s its role as an instrument.

Karaṇa-skandha.

And its natural or constitutive side.

Prakṛti-skandha.

The latter is differentiated according to the three guṇas.

Sattva.

Rajas.

Tamas.

Let’s begin with the predominantly sattvic aspect.

From this arise:

Manas.

The mind.

And the five powers of cognition.

Buddhīndriyas.

Now, manas is associated with the capacity relating to all the subtle sensory objects.

But the individual senses have more particular capacities.

Hearing is connected with sound.

Sight with visible form.

Smell with odour.

And so on.

Now here’s a subtle but crucial point.

The sense faculty is not simply identical with its physical organ.

It’s the capacity through which a particular form of cognition occurs.

And Abhinavagupta explicitly rejects an exclusively material account here.

Because the cognitive faculty is connected with the I-maker.

Think of the sentence:

Ahaṃ śṛṇomi.

I hear.

Not:

“My ear has independently manufactured some auditory data, and I’m receiving a report from it.”

No.

“I hear.”

The cognitive act is appropriated through ahaṃkāra.

And because these faculties are instruments, karaṇas, they must be connected to the agent.

Otherwise you’d need another instrument to connect the first instrument to the subject.

And another to connect that one.

And another.

Congratulations.

Infinite regress again.

We dealt with this nonsense in Chapter One.

Abhinavagupta isn’t having it.

So the faculties of cognition operate as instruments associated with limited subjectivity.

But he also makes a further distinction.

The principal powers of the limited individual are still:

Vidyā, in relation to knowledge.

And kalā, in relation to action.

And their persistence can be appreciated even in someone who lacks ordinary vision or bodily movement.

The underlying subject and its fundamental capacities aren’t simply reducible to particular working physical organs.

Now, here come the five cognitive faculties.

Śrotra

Hearing

Śabda — sound

Tvak

Touch

Sparśa — contact

Cakṣus

Sight

Rūpa — visible form

Jihvā

Taste

Rasa — flavour

Ghrāṇa

Smell

Gandha — odour

Lovely.

We’ve got a mind.

And five ways of receiving experience.

But now we need five ways of acting.

And Abhinavagupta has an unusually interesting account of why there are precisely five.

23. The five powers of action — why you can move, grab things and talk nonsense
Right.

The five action faculties are:

Karmendriyas.

But here’s something interesting.

In this account, they also arise from the sattvic aspect of ahaṃkāra, particularly where certain subtle-object capacities predominate.

Now, that’s not identical with every other Indian philosophical explanation.

And Abhinavagupta explicitly acknowledges variant classifications shortly afterward.

But first, let’s understand his own argument.

Take the act of walking.

You say:

Ahaṃ gacchāmi.

I go.

Now, the faculty of movement is associated with:

Pāda-indriya.

The power whose principal physical location is the feet.

But that doesn’t mean the metaphysical faculty is absolutely restricted to the physical feet.

A person who cannot walk may still possess capacities for movement.

And the power of movement isn’t necessarily annihilated because a particular bodily organ is damaged.

Now, Abhinavagupta says the faculties of action must be distinguished from the faculties of perception.

Because acting and apprehending aren’t identical functions.

And he invokes an interesting distinction from the Vaiśeṣika system.

Perceiving something, such as a visible form, is not classified there as an action in the same sense as moving.

So we need an account of the specific instruments of activity.

Right.

But why five?

Why not seventeen?

Or three hundred?

Well, Abhinavagupta says there are five fundamental types of practical orientation.

And the way he explains them is rather wonderful.

First: Giving something outward
You intend to expel something.

Tyāga.

Releasing.

And this corresponds to:

Pāyu.

The organ of excretion.

Now, rather unglamorous, but perfectly necessary.

Even the divine universe has apparently got to account for defecation.

No getting around it.

Second: Taking something inward
Ādāna.

Grasping.

Acquiring.

And this corresponds to:

Pāṇi.

The hands.

Imagine picking up a cup.

You intend to take something.

The hand is the principal instrument through which that activity is expressed.

Third: Both giving and taking
Movement involves a combination of these orientations.

And this corresponds to:

Pāda.

The feet.

You move from one place to another, withdrawing from one position and approaching another.

Fourth: Repose beyond that opposition
Now comes:

Upastha.

The generative or sexual faculty.

And in this classification it is associated with a form of repose beyond the simple opposition of taking and giving.

Svarūpa-viśrānti.

Resting in one’s own nature.

Now, don’t assume this means every sexual act is automatically a mystical realisation.

We’re dealing with Abhinavagupta’s functional classification of the faculty.

But notice the conceptual elegance.

Release.

Acquisition.

Their combination.

And repose beyond the pair.

Four outward orientations.

Now there’s one more.

Fifth: The inward activity of speech
Vāk.

Speech.

And this is related to the internal activity associated with vital energy.

Prāṇa.

Now, speech isn’t merely moving your mouth.

It’s the articulation of an intention through a particular faculty.

And that faculty belongs to the agency of the individual.

So we’ve got five powers of action.

Release.

Grasping.

Movement.

Generation or repose.

Speech.

Now, suppose someone objects:

“But Abhinava, I can move my hand as well as grab things with it. Shouldn’t movement be an additional hand-faculty?”

No.

Because the movement of the hand still belongs functionally to the power of movement.

The physical location of a faculty doesn’t exhaust the classification of the action.

The hand might be the principal seat of grasping, but movements occurring there can belong to the more general capacity for motion.

And because the fundamental orientations of action are five, the innumerable particular actions don’t require innumerable independent faculties.

Now here’s something interesting.

Abhinavagupta acknowledges that other authorities classify their origins differently.

Some associate the mind with rajas.

Others place the mind in sattva and the sense faculties in rajas.

And some assign rajas a connecting function.

So the details of the classificatory account vary.

But his principal argument remains:

These faculties are expressions of the limited individual’s powers of cognition and action, differentiated through ahaṃkāra.

And now we’ve established the subject’s instruments.

The mind.

The senses.

The action faculties.

But we’re still missing something.

The actual objects.

What are you going to hear?

See?

Smell?

Taste?

Touch?

That’s where the final great stage of manifestation begins.

24. Tamas produces the subtle objects of experience
Now we return to the tamasic aspect of ahaṃkāra.

The aspect associated with concealment.

And from this arise:

The five tanmātras.

Now, this is a beautiful Sanskrit word.

Tan-mātra.

Literally, something like “that alone” or “merely that.”

And in this philosophical system it refers to the subtle potential underlying a particular sensory quality.

Not yet a gross physical object.

For example:

Śabda-tanmātra.

The subtle principle of sound.

Now imagine every particular sound.

A bird singing.

Someone speaking.

A bell ringing.

Rain falling.

A gecko making some absolutely ridiculous noise behind your wall.

They’re all different.

But they possess a common characteristic.

Sound.

And Abhinavagupta says the subtle sound-principle is the undifferentiated generality preceding those particular, disturbed manifestations.

Akṣobhātmaka.

Unagitated.

Aviśeṣātmaka.

Without particular differentiation.

That’s the śabda-tanmātra.

Not one ordinary audible sound.

The subtle principle of sound as such.

And the same account applies to the other four sensory qualities.

Touch.

Form.

Taste.

Smell.

So we’ve got five tanmātras.

शब्द
Śabda

Sound

स्पर्श
Sparśa

Touch

रूप
Rūpa

Visible form

रस
Rasa

Taste

गन्ध
Gandha

Smell

And now, something happens.

The subtle principles become disturbed.

Kṣubdha.

And through that disturbance, gross elements are manifested.

This is the final stage of our descent.

We’re going from the highest Śiva-tattva all the way down to ordinary physical experience.

And Abhinavagupta gives a very precise sequence.

25. How sound becomes space, touch becomes air, and smell becomes earth
Right.

First:

Śabda-tanmātra.

The subtle potential of sound.

When it becomes active in the appropriate way, it manifests:

Ākāśa.

Space.

Or ether.

Now, why space?

Because space provides room.

Avakāśa.

The possibility of extension and location.

And sound, in this traditional framework, requires a domain in which its manifestation is possible.

So space is associated with the quality of sound.

Next:

Sparśa-tanmātra.

The subtle potential of touch.

When disturbed, it gives rise to:

Vāyu.

Air.

And the quality of sound remains associated with it because air is not separated from space.

So now we have two qualities.

Sound.

Touch.

Next:

Rūpa-tanmātra.

Visible form.

And from its disturbance arises:

Tejas.

Fire.

Now we have three associated qualities.

Sound.

Touch.

Form.

Next:

Rasa-tanmātra.

Taste.

And from its disturbance arises:

Āpas.

Water.

Four qualities.

Sound.

Touch.

Form.

Taste.

And finally:

Gandha-tanmātra.

Smell.

Its disturbance manifests:

Pṛthivī.

Earth.

And earth possesses all five of the associated sensory qualities.

Sound.

Touch.

Form.

Taste.

Smell.

Now, here’s the whole thing.

THE FIVE GROSS ELEMENTS

1

Ākāśa — Space

Sound

2

Vāyu — Air

Sound + touch

3

Tejas — Fire

Sound + touch + form

4

Āpas — Water

Sound + touch + form + taste

5

Pṛthivī — Earth

Sound + touch + form + taste + smell

Now, isn’t that fascinating?

The physical world is being analysed as an increasingly differentiated collection of sensible qualities.

Space provides the domain of sound.

Air includes touch.

Fire includes visible form.

Water includes taste.

Earth includes smell.

And every later element retains the earlier associated qualities.

Now, Abhinavagupta mentions that some other authorities explain the process somewhat differently.

They describe air arising through the combination of sound and touch, and proceed through increasingly comprehensive combinations until earth arises from all five.

Another variant of the causal arrangement.

Remember what we established at the beginning?

The conventional relationships of cause and effect can be described in different ways.

And those differences needn’t undermine the ultimate causal foundation.

But then he says something absolutely wonderful.

Something that brings us right back to the nature of substances and their properties.

26. Earth isn’t some mysterious thing hiding behind its qualities
Right.

Imagine you’ve got a lump of earth.

A stone, perhaps.

And you describe it.

It’s hard.

It’s heavy.

It has a particular colour.

A texture.

A smell.

You can interact with it through various senses.

Now, you might think:

“Well, those are all properties OF the stone.”

And Abhinavagupta asks:

What’s this additional thing behind all the properties?

Some completely separate substance that possesses them?

He says:

Guṇa-samudāya-mātram.

Merely a collection of qualities.

And:

Na anyaḥ guṇī kaścit.

There isn’t some other separate possessor of those qualities.

Now that’s fascinating.

Because he’s rejecting the necessity of an additional underlying object existing independently of the qualities through which the element is manifested.

The particular element is constituted by its relevant collection of qualities.

You don’t need to invent a mysterious second entity standing behind them.

And now we arrive at one of the most important principles in the entire cosmology.

Remember Chapter Seven?

The higher pervades the lower.

Well, he’s bringing that back.

27. The higher qualities pervade the lower
Now, within this collection of tattvas, Abhinavagupta states that the higher principle possesses a more comprehensive character.

Vyāpaka.

Pervading.

The lower principle is:

Vyāpya.

Pervaded.

And here’s how he explains the superiority of one principle over another.

A higher principle is more fundamental when the lower cannot be established without it.

Let me put that into ordinary language.

Suppose a particular manifested thing depends upon a more fundamental condition.

The more fundamental condition pervades or grounds it.

So earth is pervaded by water.

Water by fire.

Fire by air.

Air by space.

And the sequence continues upward through the subtler principles.

All the way toward Śakti.

And the supreme reality of consciousness grounds the entire process.

Now, notice something extraordinary.

We’ve arrived at exactly the principle we established at the beginning of the chapter.

The individual tattvas are not independently self-sufficient substances.

They’re particular modes of manifestation.

And their ultimate ground is the great universal luminosity.

Mahāprakāśa.

Paramaśiva.

Now think about the whole descent.

We began with unrestricted consciousness.

Then the predominance of its five powers.

Then the pure tattvas.

Then the limited individual and its fundamental impurity.

Then Māyā.

Then limited agency.

Limited knowledge.

Attachment.

Time.

Determination.

Then the individual experiencer.

Prakṛti.

The guṇas.

Intellect.

The I-maker.

The mind.

The senses.

The powers of action.

The subtle sensory qualities.

And finally the gross elements of physical experience.

The entire structure of manifested reality.

And at no point did we need to introduce a second ultimate substance standing independently outside Śiva.

The distinctions are real as manifestations within this system.

But their ultimate reality remains one.

Now here’s the really extraordinary thing.

28. The whole bloody universe is a manifestation of the I
Let’s return to that earlier distinction.

Because I think it’s the heart of the chapter.

At the level of ahaṃkāra, the limited individual appropriates an object as Self.

You think:

“I am this body.”

“I am this personality.”

“I am this particular organism with these particular capacities.”

And Abhinavagupta says that is a constructed I.

Ahaṃkāra.

But there is a more fundamental I.

The pure self-awareness of consciousness.

Aham-vimarśa.

And that doesn’t depend upon the constructed identity.

Now, this is extremely important.

Because we’re not trying to eliminate consciousness’s capacity for self-awareness.

We’re trying to recognise that its true nature is not confined to the limited identity.

So think about everything we’ve just investigated.

The body.

The senses.

The mind.

The objects.

The whole universe.

All these are expressions of consciousness.

But the limited individual has taken one particular configuration of them and declared:

“That’s me.”

And everything else:

“Not me.”

Now there’s your bondage.

The division.

The contraction.

Saṃkoca.

But suppose you recognise the underlying reality.

Suppose you recognise that the same luminosity through which your body appears also manifests the entire world.

Then the distinction between your restricted identity and what appears outside it no longer defines the ultimate nature of your Self.

And the pure I-consciousness is recognised as something fundamentally different from the constructed ego.

Not simply a bigger personality.

Not a gigantic cosmic version of yourself.

But the self-awareness of consciousness itself.

The absolute I isn’t the limited individual inflated to cosmic proportions.

It’s the unrestricted self-apprehension of the reality in which every limited individual and every object appears.

And now you can appreciate why this chapter has gone into such painstaking detail.

Why all the tattvas?

Why the guṇas?

Why the senses?

Why the distinction between māyā as an inert tattva and Māyā as divine power?

Why distinguish genuine I-consciousness from ahaṃkāra?

Because you need to understand what you’re actually identifying yourself with.

And which distinctions belong to the manifested world rather than the supreme nature of consciousness.

You cannot simply say:

“Everything is Śiva.”

And leave every underlying philosophical problem unresolved.

Abhinavagupta wants you to understand how everything is Śiva.

How the apparent many arise within the one.

How conscious and insentient aspects are distinguished.

How agency becomes limited.

How knowledge becomes particular.

How attachment arises.

How the subject appropriates a body.

And how the manifest universe, right down to its physical qualities, rests within a hierarchy ultimately grounded in supreme consciousness.

And that’s the remarkable thing.

You began with something apparently outside you.

The physical world.

And through the systematic investigation of its principles, you arrived at the consciousness through which it is manifest.

Not because you’ve escaped the world.

But because you’ve investigated its essential nature.

Tattvasvarūpa.

And here’s a final thought.

You think:

“I’m this little human being sitting in the universe.”

Abhinavagupta says:

That very thought is the result of an extraordinary series of limiting manifestations.

The actual consciousness through which you think it?

That’s not confined to the little identity you’ve constructed.

And the whole hierarchy we’ve just described?

It is an expression of the freedom of that supreme reality.

Śiva freely manifests the entire universe, including the limited individual who looks around and wonders where Śiva has gone.

The absolute bloody cheek of it.

End of Chapter Eight — Tattvasvarūpaprakāśanam.

